course: dictionary
lesson: 9854
title: Clapping syllables
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A syllable is one beat in a word. You can find the beats by clapping as you say a word: «di-no-saur» gives you three claps, so it has three syllables.
explain: Every syllable has one vowel sound. «Cat» has one beat, but «sand-wich» has two. Long words are easier to read when you read them chunk by chunk, sand-wich, not all in one go.
explain: Counting syllables helps with poems too. A haiku has three lines with five, seven and five syllables, so counting the beats keeps the rhythm right.
fact: A syllable is a beat in a word with one vowel sound.
fact: A word with one syllable, like «cat», is called a monosyllable.
fact: «Autumn» has two syllables: au-tumn. «Falling» has two: fall-ing. «Leaves» has one.
fact: In Mandarin, the tune of a syllable is part of the word, like a vowel: change the tune and you change the word.
fact: Japanese adds vowels to borrowed words, so ice cream became «aisukurīmu».
exercise: «A syllable is one beat in a word: "au-tumn" has two beats.» How many syllables does autumn have? | One | *Two | Three | Four
exercise: Clap the word «sand-wich». How many claps do you make? | Three | *Two | One | Four
exercise: Which word is a monosyllable, a word with one syllable? | Dinosaur | Falling | *Leaves | Autumn
test: A haiku has three lines of five, seven and five syllables. How many syllables are in the middle line? | Nine | Five | Three | *Seven
test: In Mandarin, "wúliáo" means bored or boring. What does "hǎo wúliáo a" mean? | I'm so happy | Let's play | *I'm so bored | Ice cream, please
exam: Why did "Merry Christmas" change into "Mele Kalikimaka" in Hawaiian? | Hawaiian people do not celebrate Christmas | *Hawaiian reshapes words to fit its sounds, and its syllables end in vowels | Hawaiian has no words for Christmas | Hawaiian uses only one syllable per word
